<chapter
 id="lecture04-10">
#title("Example: A Bit of Animation")

#para("\

I would like to present a very simple example of animation in this
chapter that makes use of higher-order functions. Please click <ulink
url=\"https://ats-lang.github.io/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/04-10/CODE/DigitClock.html\">here</ulink>
for a quick demo of this example (the demo runs the JS code generated from the
GC-era version of the example).\

")

#para("\

Intuitively, animation can be performed by calling the following
function #dyncode("animate"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
animate(fwork: () -> void): void
//
(* ****** ****** *)
//
implement
animate(fwork) =
{
  val () = fwork()
  val () = sleep_animate(100(*ms*), fwork)
}
//
')

The argument #dyncode("fwork") is supposed to be called to generate
some effect (for instance, outputing text on a webpage), and the
function call #dyncode("sleep(N)") pauses program execution for
#dyncode("N") milliseconds, where #dyncode("N") is fixed constant
(100 here). Note that #dyncode("fwork") is an envless function (the
type #stacode("() -&gt; void")): it is called again and again, and so it
cannot be a closure that is consumed by a call; state shared by the calls
is kept in a global variable.

")

#para("\

Clearly, calling #dyncode("sleep") prevents programs from being
executed concurrently. Instead, let us combine #dyncode("sleep") and
#dyncode("animate") into one function of the name #dyncode("sleep_animate"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
sleep_animate
(ms: int, fwork: () -> void): void
//
')

What #dyncode("sleep_animate(N, fwork)") does is to schedule a call to
#dyncode("animate") on #dyncode("fwork") after #dyncode("N")
milliseconds. Note that a call to #dyncode("sleep_animate") should return
immediately, causing no pause of program execution.\

")

#para("\

Let us generalize #dyncode("sleep_animate") to the function
#dyncode("execute_after"), whose interface is given as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
vtypedef
thunk = () -<lincloptr1> void
//
extern
fun
execute_after
(fwork: thunk, ms: int): void
//
')

Given a thunk #dyncode("fwork") (that is, a closure-function
returning void) and an integer #dyncode("ms"), #dyncode("execute_after")
schedules a call to #dyncode("fwork") after #dyncode("ms") milliseconds.
The thunk is created on heap and it is passed to the scheduler, which is
responsible for freeing it after the call (it is called only once): this
is the way a closure that outlives the function creating it is
handled in the absence of GC, and the type #stacode("thunk") is linear.
With #dyncode("execute_after"), the function #dyncode("sleep_animate")
can be implemented as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
implement
sleep_animate(ms, fwork) =
execute_after(llam() => animate(fwork), ms)
//
')

where #keycode("llam") is used for creating a linear closure that is called at
most once.

")

#para("\

If we target JS, then the function #dyncode("execute_after") can be
implemented by calling the native function #dyncode("setTimeout") of JS
(through #keycode("$extfcall")); in JS the closure is an ordinary function
value and the JS engine reclaims it. For the C code of this book, the file
#filename("eventloop.hats") in the code of this chapter implements
#dyncode("execute_after") for a tiny simulated event loop (a clock and one
pending event, which is a linear thunk stored as a pointer), so that the
examples can run without a browser: the call to
#dyncode("eventloop_run(limit)") calls the pending thunk, and
then frees it, until no event is pending or the due time is beyond
#dyncode("limit"). The pending thunk that is not called is freed, too, and
the programs run clean with the address sanitizer.

")

#para("\

The following program animates a counter (an event is a printed line):

#dats2xhtml('\
var theCount_var: int = 0
val theCount =
ref_make_viewptr{int}(view@ theCount_var | addr@ theCount_var)

fun
tick(): void = let
  val n = !theCount
in
  !theCount := n + 1;
  println! ("tick ", n, " at ", eventloop_now(), "ms")
end // end of [tick]
//
val () = animate(lam() => tick())
val () = eventloop_run(450)
//
')

and it prints the lines #dyncode("tick 0 at 0ms"),
#dyncode("tick 1 at 100ms"), ..., #dyncode("tick 4 at 400ms"). Note that
a reference can only be created from memory that is never freed (a toplevel
variable in this case) and the content of the reference is not linear.
For a digital clock directly based on #dyncode("animate"), please see
the source of the GC-era demo <ulink
url=\"https://github.com/ats-lang/ats-lang.github.io/blob/master/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/04-10/CODE/DigitClock.dats\">DigitClock.dats</ulink>.

")

#para("\

Given N, fwork, and ms, the following function
#dyncode("repeat_execute_after") executes the calls
#dyncode("fwork(i)") for i ranging from 0 to N-1 and then repeats:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
repeat_execute_after
( N: int,
  fwork: (int) -> void, ms: int) : void
//
implement
repeat_execute_after
(
N, fwork, ms
) = let
//
fun
auxmain(i: int): void =
if i >= N
  then auxmain(0)
  else (fwork(i); execute_after(llam() => auxmain(i+1), ms))
//
in
  auxmain(0)
end // end of [repeat_execute_after]
//
')

For any two consecutive calls to #dyncode("fwork"), there is a delay
of #dyncode("ms") milliseconds between them. In a case where a call to
#dyncode("fwork") draws an image, calling
#dyncode("repeat_execute_after") may create an animation effect of
some sort. For example, please click <ulink
url=\"https://ats-lang.github.io/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/04-10/CODE/Sierpinski.html\">here</ulink>
to see an animated version of the example of Sierpinkski triangle
presented previously.

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/04-10/CODE", "on-line") the entirety
of the code used in this chapter (the file #filename("animate.dats") contains
the code of this chapter, which was compiled and run with
#command("patscc") and with the address sanitizer, no leak is reported).
The mentioned URL link(s) can be found as follows:

